6 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048992 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428987 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048992 #edb/ref 1031 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Real Distinct Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048992 #edb/ref 1033 3239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048992 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d1b1f9a6-d57b-5e8a-a131-1da2ca6d5c52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429547 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1031 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Zero Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1033 6379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049942 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "20144df7-61f2-5b88-ac4e-f707b4ea5302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428764 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428765 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1033 2818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050450 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "49ef522d-8ba6-502a-8a64-c19ff9a6b681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186221416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1031 "Shifted Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1033 6380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050780 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "660c3818-8c01-5108-b77f-70c734343bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1031 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1033 3240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050967 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "764fe6dd-703b-527f-a798-104e24a81888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221169 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221169 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=-5,$ with corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [3, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-2, \\ 1]^T,$ respectively. Using this information, sketch the phase portrait of the system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6649e376-1865-5cb9-8892-687f80082d22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221169 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/b6/b6e1ba9b197e105bfa8599f81ef6a15381cf2d996f75528733b8bf5f32ed1f66.svg)\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/c8/c83c3bdb26e609cf9e5d023ea9e4f4bf366162dc9957972355b0eaab06c42a44.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c39f92243806a8682701374244fa2f7def32ab56ae509c920a31e75abfacb88f.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/bf/bf953962efa5f9c6f59878328bd99be9f795d31e05069e8925b520a48eedc3c7.svg) has the equation \n$y=- \\dfrac12x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/0e/0eec91967012c45aadd7ed416606e7ff0f5d9fdbfe7a9a3b75a24108a6160d00.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{-5t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/96/96a198c7fe3d98d3775ffc176b6659022b18676caf849cbc8d5c66833113bb19.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74b573da705aafba25c74aa789dc3a51464683dd5a47a999ba9c557d35ed1d02.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221170 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221170 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e89c64cb-e046-592c-929b-9269cb7b2758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221170 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/b0/b0b5abdfe48d45a352b101e134ebb06165824385c1162a705e539f911af9f267.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/96/962887c2e859c2afadfc122bd5ecc22fe28eb354b348c5153c16577de5fe37d8.svg) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](images/9b/9bbb4470407c8498cf3270e247e7b9a9d214bd1ff1b3d85a75d17189bee26ea9.svg)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/55/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/2f/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221171 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221171 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edb1633f-44e1-5324-8048-80252c65afe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221171 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/9d/9d8df402a06960cd5f2e0c772a5fab345e4610cc90c7e88202dab34f2ab97e65.svg)\nwhere ![](images/58/58ab8470c661bcc5f0c95df2640185f95fc9733c1f4bf718ce51dba7a0b9a989.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y=0 \\\\ 5x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+2y=0\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n2y=-2x\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n![](images/b9/b9059ea5506ed372cb5b21771b177966be2c607934164b2ad16466a31d423834.svg)\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221405 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221406 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186222000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221407 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1045 "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Zero Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186222300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221471 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "29e78799-50b0-5ee7-8c3d-1f30ea789765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221951 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "059b4cf7-aecd-509d-b95b-c42c513302b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221951 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/80/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222300 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222300 #edb/ref 1061 836100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222300 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e26b239-0214-5bf7-b43f-2eee2d68bd35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222300 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222301 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222301 #edb/ref 1061 836101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222301 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3674acde-de47-5169-8c61-17c706504d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222301 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222302 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222302 #edb/ref 1061 836104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222302 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d39a58a3-4d07-53e9-9513-1e0afa489a46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222302 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222303 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222303 #edb/ref 1061 836105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222303 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5eb0a853-1f4a-5077-9fde-a01f3a87b01f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222303 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222304 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222304 #edb/ref 1061 836108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e2413a3-578e-5d30-80ac-4779d0c2e5e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222305 #edb/ref 1061 836109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "930d788a-1c62-5ebd-b158-c4759f94afa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222484 #edb/ref 1064 "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/6f/6f6a92e2b8fb89f833268a5526876cb3af5215db0193a2aaaad8aac5790752ea.svg)\nwhen the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at least one *zero eigenvalue*.\n\nA zero eigenvalue is special because $e^{0\\cdot t}=1,$ so the corresponding component of a solution has *no exponential growth or decay*. Geometrically, this often creates a **line of equilibrium points** (infinitely many fixed points), instead of a single equilibrium at the origin.\n\nIndeed, equilibrium points satisfy $\\mathbf{x}'=\\mathbf{0},$ so for $\\mathbf{x}'=A\\mathbf{x}$ we solve\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThat is, equilibrium points form the *null space* of $A.$\n\n- If $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the null space is $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ and the only equilibrium is the origin.\n\n- If $\\det(A)=0,$ then the null space contains infinitely many points (typically a line through the origin in $2\\times2$ cases).\n\nA zero eigenvalue guarantees $\\det(A)=0$, so in that case the null space is nontrivial.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222484 #edb/ref 1065 14475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222484 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c770b47f-91af-50ab-b4ff-900448268523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222484 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222487 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has a repeated zero eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (multiplicity $2$). Here, the vector field carries no exponential growth or decay in any direction.\nThere are two subcases.\n\n- If there are two independent eigenvectors (we have a zero matrix), then every direction behaves like an equilibrium direction, and solutions are constant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(0).\n$$\n\nSo *every point is an equilibrium*, and the phase portrait is a field of zero vectors.\n\n- If there is only one eigenvector (we have a singular nonzero matrix), then we use a generalized eigenvector $\\mathbf{w}$ with $(A-0\\cdot I)\\mathbf{w}=A\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{v}$, and solutions look like\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}+c_2\\bigl(t\\,\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\bigr), \n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nNotice that here\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}_E=c_1\\mathbf{v}$ still gives a *line of equilibrium points*, while\n\n- other trajectories align with the eigenspace structure, yielding a **shear flow** where solutions grow linearly with $t$ (algebraic growth).\n\nLet's see a concrete example of the second case." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222487 #edb/ref 1065 14480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222487 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f3da1055-bac8-5f6a-952c-fda37413939d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222487 #edb/ref 1067 "Systems With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222488 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors ![](images/47/47c0d53d1eed7289cee7d61cfaf1e8a62bccc084f445b5ca217c9b3bec78b0d8.svg) and ![](images/99/998a77eb9b491e2d1e733a0dfe1a65cc1d39759650e36c671ca1c2aa1064e29a.svg) respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/e7/e71f95804ab65c6d60a907f33967a744b13e2c977ce4d518740a8470cc3a4e06.svg)\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/71/715228dc0a53bd16e4f6b055c9a4bc4679d8761e7a461e61f58003ffa8563e42.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/47/4734b2299d6f70fec85b91c2d6e74332ed722dc7522d497ef8bb4e80682d3052.svg) has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/4f/4f83cfd4f18ee670366bc8987a4c87b900ab9f5226fec8d18440627a0db6c6d0.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222488 #edb/ref 1065 14481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222488 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "16390489-4623-562e-a8a2-f603efe07e9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222488 #edb/ref 1067 "Systems With One Zero Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302530 #edb/ref 1080 "images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0adb1db8-6564-5d3a-98a9-63a8577d6a7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302882 #edb/ref 1080 "images/1b/1bf19c8cc89bd745c5515570921e1db00b552d351459ccd2e17921d63c4fa3d7.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302882 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302882 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4380720f-1f8d-546c-8c6c-7f3e30b7ae37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186303626 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2e/2e18d0b6def07666f9eaf4f4d