92186307174 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da4b3bdf-1f79-5acb-9f40-db982a1bfa54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307342 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307342 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307342 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee88a534-7d05-5f3f-8c60-c3935e9b3a5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307909 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7b46cd2e-0392-50d7-bcaf-b0944ec48370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308022 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9e4c7528-85a6-56a0-a678-684c0edfdcb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308274 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eec63233-efc6-5468-9d0d-3bdbfeffc991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333782" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308503 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85e6fc84-64ed-533c-94e3-94bfdcc415ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308503 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308515 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b9dc234-d83d-52ed-858a-43cf39227d57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308515 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308661 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308661 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308661 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308661 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-9x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }3]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[-\\frac{1}{3},\\text{ }0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fbf3cd8-344d-52ae-9b3c-567d393e20b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308661 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a70188cf5de7e7b64530aa661447b7fa7fc1e918ca3de41088e67274d813b7a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=9t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/37/37deb5a0fda7ef15a016ec2d73a3b935074b6696184dd480ebe5b858ce517500.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=-9t-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/60/60b49680cf94888122f25432586a3546dea206ae960f951afd187444afc8881a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7b4de40b147f1745986476ab0b0cbad22ad0484924f62389c6c19187d5d06245.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429547 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429547 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: differential=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: en